      "id": "76",
      "type": "related_content",
      "attributes": {
        "id": 76,
        "relationship_type": "related",
        "body": "The dictionary form of an う-verb (e.g., 買う) is the base form used to describe current, habitual, or future actions (e.g., 買う = 'I buy' or 'I will buy'). In contrast, the past form (-た form) expresses completed actions or past states (e.g., 買った = 'I bought'). Both forms commonly appear in grammar patterns that require the casual/plain form, such as ～と思います ('I think').",
        "first_relatable": {
          "id": 26,
          "slug": "う-Verbs",
          "title": "う-Verb (Dictionary)",
          "meaning": "う + ます (Godan Verbs)",
          "level": "JLPT5",
          "type_pascal": "GrammarPoint",
          "type_snake": "grammar_point"
        },
        "second_relatable": {
          "id": 41,
          "slug": "う-verb-past",
          "title": "う-Verb (Past)",
          "meaning": "う - Verbs (Past tense)",
          "level": "JLPT5",
          "type_pascal": "GrammarPoint",
          "type_snake": "grammar_point"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "77",
      "type": "related_content",
      "attributes": {
        "id": 77,
        "relationship_type": "related",
        "body": "The dictionary form of an う-verb (e.g., 買う) is its base form, describing non-past actions (e.g., 買う = 'I buy' or 'I will buy'). It's used casually or as part of other grammatical patterns. In contrast, the て-form (verb + て) connects verbs to show sequences or to form requests and various other grammar structures (e.g., 買ってください = 'Please buy...').",
        "first_relatable": {
          "id": 26,
          "slug": "う-Verbs",
          "title": "う-Verb (Dictionary)",
          "meaning": "う + ます (Godan Verbs)",
          "level": "JLPT5",
          "type_pascal": "GrammarPoint",
          "type_snake": "grammar_point"
        },
        "second_relatable": {
          "id": 416,
          "slug": "verb-て",
          "title": "Verb + て",
          "meaning": "And, Then (Linking events)",
          "level": "JLPT5",
          "type_pascal": "GrammarPoint",
          "type_snake": "grammar_point"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "45",
      "type": "related_content",
      "attributes": {
        "id": 45,
        "relationship_type": "related",
        "body": "る-Verbs and う-Verbs are the two main verb types in Japanese. Their dictionary forms are the basic forms used in dictionaries and casual non-past sentences (e.g., 食べる = 'I eat' or 'I will eat'). The difference between these verb types is in their conjugation patterns, not their meaning or nuance. Be careful: not all verbs ending in る are る-Verbs (for example, 帰る 'to return' is actually an う-Verb).",
        "first_relatable": {
          "id": 12,
          "slug": "る-Verbs",
          "title": "る-Verb (Dictionary)",
          "meaning": "る + ます (Ichidan Verbs)",
          "level": "JLPT5",
          "type_pascal": "GrammarPoint",
          "type_snake": "grammar_point"
        },
        "second_relatable": {
          "id": 26,
          "slug": "う-Verbs",
          "title": "う-Verb (Dictionary)",
          "meaning": "う + ます (Godan Verbs)",
          "level": "JLPT5",
          "type_pascal": "GrammarPoint",
          "type_snake": "grammar_point"
        }
      }
